sitive or negative view all  the time um  people really began to just reject her  and whenever she was rejected she tended  to be even more outrageous in her  behavior  one typical scene and it comes you know  she had a great way of mimicking so  people wouldn't miss the point she went  into a bingo  game in a church and did the christ  thing from the new testament went in and  tipped over the tables and started  screaming at the top of her lungs in  order to make the point this was sort of  typical and they did distant that many  people did distance herself from  themselves from her so  many times the americans who are  perceived in their own country are  admired and revered abroad for example  woody allen in france marilyn monroe  jerry lewis  roman polanski well he was a european  though how do you have any information  about how she was accepted abroad  uh chastised here an outsider but was  she respected elsewhere  she actually started the international  atheist association she started herself  and she was she was fairly well regarded  abroad  she tended to behave a little better  when she went abroad and i think that  helped too and also  people were less  obsessed  with atheists in the movement abroad so  she could go as she did  to sweden denmark india  france many places and meet with  atheists and no one really cared  you know what they didn't you didn't  bring about the reaction that it did in  the united states so she didn't have  that kind of visibility but she seems to  have been fairly highly regarded enough  to be elected the president of that  group for several years so she did  pretty well abroad  i have a question to go back to the  diaries  i'm always interested in documentation  of scholarship and so on how did you  gain access to the diaries  and did you have access to  documents that american atheists keep or  or even documents of her enemies and so  forth  um let me answer the first question  about the diaries the diaries were when  they were found were actually sold  at an auction to help pay off the  organization's debts they were purchased  by an attorney  from austin  and it took me about a year to persuade  him  for reasons which i'm still not quite  sure to let me use to read  the diaries and i'm very grateful  i did not intend to include much of  madeleine's personal life  in this book i really wanted to look at  atheism in cold war america as an issue  with madeleine as a representation of  that issue  most of the stuff that had been written  about madeleine was pretty  antagonistic  um and what i found  from my research most of it was wrong  and that's why i had to in a sense  retreat and i was also  constantly challenged by this idea of  why did she do it and i really took the  diaries to help me figure that out um  also purchased as part of the diaries  were many boxes of personal documents  from her life  which was another one birth certificates  that's another story read the chapter in  the book on birth certificates because  she not she would have one birth  certificate for her son william  which was probably the original one then  she went and had a second one done with  different names  and she did the same thing when her  second son  was born and she was constantly  playing if you will these kinds of games  but the documentation is really there's  quite a bit now as far as american  atheists are concerned  i was not very successful  they were very protective  of her i think i would have had better  luck if she hadn't disappeared when i  was writing the book  and then they were under such siege  thinking that she'd absconded with the  money that they sort of drew up the  drawbridge and decided they weren't  going to let anyone  near that documentation however having  said that i it worked out okay because  really there isn't much personal  material an american atheist there's a  lot of literature it's a wonderful  collection on atheism  madeleine collected it but there wasn't  a lot of personal papers one thing about  madeleine madeleine wrote everything  down and got it published she had  newsletters she had magazines she had a  radio show and then published  transcripts in the radio shows so there  wasn't much that madeleine thought or  said it didn't make it into print so  yes if i can follow on that question  uh was madeleine  spiritual at all did she believe in  spirituality  and in examining her diaries  do you  perceive it all over time any kind of  spiritual or even emotional growth going  on  oh  interesting ending to that question um  i didn't detect very much of anything in  terms of a spiritual understanding  there's just nothing there  to deal with she was a committed as she  believed rationalist who just didn't  believe in any kind of spiritual  focus or force in the world  i didn't see  very much there at all  um  and i think if anything  rather than looking at it in terms of  spiritual growth i think if anything  whatever  spiritual  feeling she might have had  um  were lost  over time she died or you know her last  few she was a very very bitter angry  woman at the end  okay  you mentioned in europe that she could  become well behaved  i wonder  was there anything endearing or soft  about this woman or was it a show  i mean  understanding a little bit about the  atheist leaders now in the organizations  it's a plethora of people being shunned  and splitting off and so it seems more  normal right to me so i was just  wondering is it is this harshness just  part of a show or  i think to a large extent it was  it was it was her performance so that  she would be noticed  but she was a pretty  outspoken woman and even those who liked  her  said that in private she was pretty  tough to be around  she didn't tolerate fools  well at all and if you said something  she didn't agree with she let you know  there was no question on the other hand  they say she had a fabulous sense of  humor  and  you know could be  fun to be around but those people were  pretty few and far between i think the  frustration came out you know with her  over the years now  you mentioned europe i think one of the  reasons again i i want to emphasize that  i believe that she was  better behaved in europe is because she  didn't have the spotlight to misbehave  they didn't care that she was there so  there's another atheist group it just  wasn't a big issue  in europe one of the things that kind of  ignited her in the united states was  that she wherever she went the spotlight  was on her and  she would seize it by behaving the way  she did um i suppose she could have  engaged in some outrageous behavior  abroad but she just didn't it wasn't  there  in your  reading of her diaries and in your  discussion of that early period of her  life and her own comment about  i believe it was in one in a later  period of her life where she wondered  to herself why she always wanted to be  angry and and against something  it sounded as though you might be  implying that she was or portraying her  as  a rebel with the cause  by nature that that was her sort of  fundamental personality bent and  further that that perhaps more than  anything more than particular rational  beliefs might be what was driving her  and i just wonder if you would  comment further on that if that is what  you're implying in the book and if she  ever herself seemed to if that is the  case  did she ever herself seem to come to  some kind of an underst self  understanding  about  what was um  making her tick in terms of why she  needed to be  always against something to thrive  yeah clearly there was a rebellious  nature there and she knew it  i think to a certain extent  anyone in that situation must have had  moments of loneliness when she wished  she wasn't the way that she was because  she led i mean it was a pretty desperate  life  i didn't go into it but the two or three  years after the murray case  um she had to have guards  her house was stoned windows broken  tires slashed her sons were beat up  william was thrown before a bus i mean  it was pretty tough going and i'm sure  that she had moments  when she wished things were different  um  but i think she she was either worked  into put herself into a situation where  she had nothing else that she could do  but fight back  and one could put a gendered  interpretation of what's going on but i  really do see this as a woman  in the early 50s  very bright  very well educated who made some  mistakes and never got over it and  decided rather than apologizing for it  she was going to pull the system down  around her  i really think that was a lot of the  motivation  okay well thank you very much i  appreciate it  [Music]  brian lebeau is author of the atheist  madeline murray o'hare it's published by  new york university press on the web at  nyupress.org  next week on public lives a new  biography of andrew jackson from andrew  burstein
 

